+FROM golang:1.27.4-alpine AS build
+WORKDIR /app
+COPY go.mod go.sum ./
+RUN go mod download
+COPY main.go fortunes.html ./
+RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -trimpath -ldflags="-s -w" -o server .
+
+FROM alpine:3.23
+COPY --from=build /app/server /server
+EXPOSE 8080 8081 8082 8443
+CMD ["/server"]
diff --git a/frameworks/celeris/README.md b/frameworks/celeris/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5c94448
--- /dev/null
+++ b/frameworks/celeris/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+# celeris
+
+[celeris](https://github.com/goceleris/celeris) is an HTTP engine and router for Go with an adaptive io_uring/epoll event loop on Linux. This entry is a framework entry (`type: emerging`, `mode: standard`): routes, middleware, query binding and JSON go through celeris's documented APIs.
+
+## Two engines serve this entry
+
+celeris is cleartext by design: its io_uring and epoll engines speak HTTP/1.1 and h2c only and have no TLS (celeris README, "TLS"). The entry therefore runs two kinds of listener, and the rows measure different engines:
+
+| Port | Protocol | celeris engine | Profiles |
+|------|----------|----------------|----------|
+| 8080 | HTTP/1.1 and WebSocket | **Adaptive** (io_uring/epoll), the default | baseline, pipelined, limited-conn, async, latency-1m, latency-10k, json-comp, async-db, fortunes, echo-ws, echo-ws-pipeline, echo-ws-limited |
+| 8082 | h2c prior knowledge (HTTP/1.1 kept on the port) | **Adaptive** (io_uring/epoll), the default | baseline-h2c, json-h2c |
+| 8081 | HTTP/1.1 over TLS (ALPN `http/1.1`) | **std engine** behind a `crypto/tls` listener | json-tls, 8gbit |
+| 8443 | h2 over TLS (ALPN `h2`, `http/1.1`) | **std engine** behind a `crypto/tls` listener | baseline-h2, static-h2 |
+
+The TLS rows are std-engine (`net/http` underneath) performance, which is what celeris documents for in-process HTTPS (`Engine: celeris.Std` with `StartWithListener(tls.NewListener(ln, cfg))`). They are not the headline engine's numbers. The 8081/8443 listeners open only when the harness mounts `/certs` (it does so for the TLS and h2 profiles), and they serve the certificate pair mounted at `/certs/server.crt` and `/certs/server.key` (TLS 1.2 minimum, so TLS 1.3 is negotiated with modern clients).
+
+## Not subscribed, and why
+
+- `connections-1m`, `connections-1m-tls`: not subscribed. Memory arithmetic for the 59.5 GiB preview host (GUESS marks my estimates): the generator alone holds about 41 GB for `connections-1m` (zrk, ~41 KiB per connection, profiles.sh:97-98) or about 19 GiB for the TLS one (common.sh), and on the TLS side the std engine keeps a goroutine, a `tls.Conn` and `net/http` buffers per connection (GUESS 30-50 KiB, i.e. 30-50 GB at a million), so the TLS case is about 49-69 GiB against 59.5 GiB: it does not fit with margin; the cleartext case is 41 GB plus the engine's per-connection state. celeris at a million connections has not been tried.
+- `static-tls`: reference-only for frameworks; skipped to keep the board entry small (the same handler serves it, so it can be added).
+- `baseline-h3`, `static-h3`, `unary-grpc`, `unary-grpc-tls`, `gateway-*`, `production-stack`: celeris has no HTTP/3, no gRPC and no compose stack.
+
+## Handlers
+
+| Endpoint | Implementation |
+|----------|----------------|
+| `GET /pipeline` | `c.String` |
+| `GET/POST /baseline11`, `GET /baseline2` | `c.QueryInt` for `a` and `b`, `c.Body()` for the POST body (Content-Length and chunked), `c.String` |
+| `GET /delay/:ms` | `Route.Async()` (the wait parks a goroutine, not a loop thread) |
+| `GET /json/:count` | `c.JSON`, compression by the `middleware/compress` default configuration (the middleware negotiates `Accept-Encoding`: it answers gzip to `gzip, br`) |
+| `POST /echo` | `c.Body()` back with `c.Blob` |
+| `GET /static/*` (TLS servers only) | `middleware/static` with `Compress: true` (serves the pre-compressed `.br`/`.gz` siblings) |
+| `GET /async-db` | `Route.Async()`, celeris's first-party `driver/postgres` pool (`postgres.Open`, `WithEngine`, `WithMaxOpen(DATABASE_MAX_CONN - 8)`) |
+| `GET /fortunes` | `Route.Async()`, same pool, `html/template` (embedded `fortunes.html`, auto-escaping), bytewise sort |
+| `GET /ws` | `middleware/websocket`, `ReadMessageReuse`/`WriteMessage` echo (the README's documented echo loop); a plain GET answers 426 |
+
+Notes:
+
+- Default configuration. `Config.GCPercent` and `Config.SocketBusyPoll` stay off (both are opt-in in celeris and neither is used here).
+- One async-db detail: scanning a `jsonb` column straight into a `*[]byte` or `*string` returns the binary wire form with its leading version byte (`0x01`) on the celeris version pinned here, so `tags` is scanned through a `database/sql`-style `Scanner` type, which decodes correctly. This is a celeris driver defect, not a harness issue.
+- The second `Adaptive` server on 8082 shares the cpuset with the one on 8080; both are idle when only one port is loaded.
+
+## Build
+
+`golang:1.27.4-alpine` build stage, `alpine:3.23` runtime; module versions are pinned in `go.mod`/`go.sum` (celeris `v1.5.12-0.20261120120335-fe2b5be71ebc` = main `fe2b5be71ebc`, `middleware/compress v1.5.11`).
